window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "What Needs Interpreting? — Exercise 1", "chapter": "What Needs Interpreting?", "url": "01-measurement-problem.html#exercise-1", "text": "Compute the pointer probabilities for α=√3/2 and β=i/2. They are 3/4 and 1/4; the relative phase does not change these Z statistics."}, {"title": "What Needs Interpreting? — Exercise 2", "chapter": "What Needs Interpreting?", "url": "01-measurement-problem.html#exercise-2", "text": "Find the reduced system purity for general normalized α,β. The purity is |α|⁴+|β|⁴. It is one only when one amplitude vanishes."}, {"title": "What Needs Interpreting? — Exercise 3", "chapter": "What Needs Interpreting?", "url": "01-measurement-problem.html#exercise-3", "text": "Why does the measurement interaction not clone an arbitrary qubit? The tensor square has cross terms αβ|01⟩ and αβ|10⟩ and squared amplitudes. The correlated output copies basis labels, not arbitrary states."}, {"title": "What Needs Interpreting? — Exercise 4", "chapter": "What Needs Interpreting?", "url": "01-measurement-problem.html#exercise-4", "text": "List one commitment that a collapse model changes. Universal unitary evolution is modified by stochastic localization; single outcomes are then associated with an additional physical process."}, {"title": "What Needs Interpreting? — Exercise 5", "chapter": "What Needs Interpreting?", "url": "01-measurement-problem.html#exercise-5", "text": "Explain why tracing out a system is not a physical collapse. The partial trace changes which observables we describe, leaving the global state untouched. Collapse would change the state or its physical interpretation."}, {"title": "What Needs Interpreting? — Exercise 6", "chapter": "What Needs Interpreting?", "url": "01-measurement-problem.html#exercise-6", "text": "What additional value rule is used in the three-claim tension? A definite record is represented by a state in its corresponding pointer subspace. Changing the relation between state and definite properties changes the argument."}, {"title": "What Needs Interpreting? — Exercise 7", "chapter": "What Needs Interpreting?", "url": "01-measurement-problem.html#exercise-7", "text": "For E±=(I±ηZ)/2, determine allowed real η. The eigenvalues are (1+η)/2 and (1−η)/2, so −1≤η≤1; their sum gives I."}, {"title": "What Needs Interpreting? — Exercise 8", "chapter": "What Needs Interpreting?", "url": "01-measurement-problem.html#exercise-8", "text": "Calculate p(+) for ρ=(I+rZ)/2 and E+ above. The probability is (1+ηr)/2. It depends on both the state and the measurement sharpness."}, {"title": "What Needs Interpreting? — Exercise 9", "chapter": "What Needs Interpreting?", "url": "01-measurement-problem.html#exercise-9", "text": "Why are effects insufficient for predicting a second measurement? The effects fix outcome probabilities but not conditional output states. The disturbance map is needed for subsequent statistics."}, {"title": "What Needs Interpreting? — Exercise 10", "chapter": "What Needs Interpreting?", "url": "01-measurement-problem.html#exercise-10", "text": "Design a comparison rubric for two interpretations. Specify the state meaning, ontology, dynamics, outcome account, probability rule, and empirical domain for each. Compare the same apparatus model before discussing explanatory preferences."}, {"title": "A detector is a quantum system", "chapter": "What Needs Interpreting?", "url": "01-measurement-problem.html#section-1-1", "text": "1.1 A detector is a quantum system 1.1.1 Follow the amplitudes into the apparatus Imagine that you prepare a qubit in \\(|+\\rangle=(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\). You measure Z, write down a result, and repeat. Half the records are approximately zero and half one. Nothing mysterious has happened to the statistics. The difficulty begins when we ask the quantum theory to describe the device making those records. Represent its ready state by \\(|A_r\\rangle\\), with distinguishable records \\(|A_0\\rangle,|A_1\\rangle\\). An ideal interaction satisfies \\(U|0,A_r\\rangle=|0,A_0\\rangle\\) and \\(U|1,A_r\\rangle=|1,A_1\\rangle\\). The same linear operator must therefore satisfy \\[U(\\alpha|0\\rangle+\\beta|1\\rangle)|A_r\\rangle=\\alpha|0,A_0\\rangle+\\beta|1,A_1\\rangle.\\] We have derived a correlation, not a randomly selected term. The detector is perfectly reliable within each component: there is no amplitude for a zero paired with the wrong record. Reliability and uniqueness are different questions. The state has exactly the correlations an experiment needs, while its relation to one observed record still calls for an account. A pointer qubit initially in |0⟩ makes this concrete: a CNOT produces α|00⟩+β|11⟩. This is an ideal premeasurement. A stable laboratory record additionally involves amplification and interaction with an environment. Neither step should be silently replaced with a collapse postulate. An ideal premeasurement correlates two quantum systems. Time runs left to right; a filled control dot and circled target denote CNOT. No measurement box or collapse is hidden in the gate. Worked check 1.1. For α=β=1/√2, is the output a product? Reasoned solution No. Its reduced qubit state is I/2, which has purity 1/2. A bipartite pure product would have a pure reduced state. The output is entangled."}, {"title": "Three claims in tension", "chapter": "What Needs Interpreting?", "url": "01-measurement-problem.html#section-1-2", "text": "1.2 Three claims in tension 1.2.1 State exactly where the problem enters Let us make the tension precise rather than calling everything “weird.” Consider three commitments: the wavefunction is a complete physical description; every isolated system, including an apparatus, evolves linearly and unitarily; and a completed measurement has one definite outcome in the ordinary sense of a unique macroscopic record. With the additional usual link between a definite pointer value and the corresponding pointer subspace, the state above does not satisfy all three commitments at once for nonzero α and β. This is a formulation of the measurement problem, not a theorem declaring quantum mechanics contradictory. One can change the account of completeness, the dynamics, the account of outcomes, or the role assigned to the quantum state. Interpretations differ partly because they reject different parts of this package. \\[\\rho_A=\\operatorname{Tr}_S|\\Psi\\rangle\\langle\\Psi|=|\\alpha|^2|A_0\\rangle\\langle A_0|+|\\beta|^2|A_1\\rangle\\langle A_1|.\\] The pointer's density operator gives the correct local frequencies. It does not license the further inference that the joint state has secretly become one product state or the other. A partial trace is an operation on a description of a subsystem. It is not a new dynamical law acting on a single experimental run. This distinction will do much of the work in the next chapter. Worked check 1.2. Does a diagonal pointer state identify the result in an individual run? Reasoned solution It supplies probabilities for pointer measurements. An individual outcome requires whatever event, conditioning, configuration, or branch account the chosen interpretation provides."}, {"title": "Predictions and physical accounts", "chapter": "What Needs Interpreting?", "url": "01-measurement-problem.html#section-1-3", "text": "1.3 Predictions and physical accounts 1.3.1 Separate the formalism from an explanation There are several questions hiding inside “What does the wavefunction mean?” Is it a physical field, part of a law, a summary of information, or an agent's probability assignment? Which quantities are definite? What makes a record stable? Why should squared amplitudes govern expectations? Asking these separately makes disagreements easier to locate. Our common operational language uses a preparation ρ, a measurement with positive effects \\(E_k\\) summing to I, and \\(p(k)=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho E_k)\\). To predict later experiments we also need an instrument: completely positive maps \\(\\mathcal I_k\\), with \\(\\sum_k\\mathcal I_k\\) trace preserving. The conditional state is \\(\\mathcal I_k(\\rho)/p(k)\\) when the outcome has nonzero probability. Effects alone do not specify disturbance. An interpretation must connect these calculations to a clear account of experiments. A modified theory can also change the predictions; objective-collapse models are a central example. We will not rank approaches by how strange their names sound. We will ask what each posits, what equations it uses, which probabilities it reproduces, and which explanatory task remains open. Keep an assumption ledger as you read. Write “calculation,” “postulate,” “theorem under assumptions,” or “interpretive proposal” next to each important step. This habit is more valuable than memorizing slogans about observers. Worked check 1.3. Can two instruments share the same effects? Reasoned solution Yes. Measuring Z and retaining |k⟩, or measuring Z and then preparing |+⟩, both have effects |k⟩⟨k|. Their later X statistics differ: the first gives equal outcomes, the second gives + with certainty."}, {"title": "Decoherence and the Classical Record — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Decoherence and the Classical Record", "url": "02-decoherence.html#exercise-1", "text": "For α=β=1/√2 and real γ, find the eigenvalues of the reduced matrix. They are (1+γ)/2 and (1−γ)/2; positivity requires |γ|≤1."}, {"title": "Decoherence and the Classical Record — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Decoherence and the Classical Record", "url": "02-decoherence.html#exercise-2", "text": "For γ=i/2, find the reduced-state purity. For equal populations, purity is (1+|γ|²)/2=5/8. The phase changes the Bloch direction, not its length."}, {"title": "Decoherence and the Classical Record — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Decoherence and the Classical Record", "url": "02-decoherence.html#exercise-3", "text": "How many independent fragments with g=1/2 reduce the overlap below 10⁻³? N=10 suffices since 2⁻¹⁰≈0.0009766; N=9 does not."}, {"title": "Decoherence and the Classical Record — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Decoherence and the Classical Record", "url": "02-decoherence.html#exercise-4", "text": "Which basis does X⊗B monitor? The X eigenstates |+⟩ and |−⟩ acquire different environmental records and are unchanged as system states by this interaction alone."}, {"title": "Decoherence and the Classical Record — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Decoherence and the Classical Record", "url": "02-decoherence.html#exercise-5", "text": "Does damping off-diagonal elements specify a trajectory for one run? No. An ensemble map constrains averages. A trajectory requires a measurement scheme or additional dynamical postulates."}, {"title": "Decoherence and the Classical Record — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Decoherence and the Classical Record", "url": "02-decoherence.html#exercise-6", "text": "Calculate the X expectation after dephasing time t for |+⟩. It is exp(−Γt). Z remains zero and the state approaches I/2 as t grows."}, {"title": "Decoherence and the Classical Record — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Decoherence and the Classical Record", "url": "02-decoherence.html#exercise-7", "text": "Compute the ZZ expectation in the Bell state and the mixture. It is +1 in both, so ZZ does not detect their coherence difference."}, {"title": "Decoherence and the Classical Record — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Decoherence and the Classical Record", "url": "02-decoherence.html#exercise-8", "text": "Can a local measurement distinguish these two global states? No. Both reduced states are I/2, hence every local POVM has identical probabilities."}, {"title": "Decoherence and the Classical Record — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Decoherence and the Classical Record", "url": "02-decoherence.html#exercise-9", "text": "Apply inverse premeasurement to the mixture. The result is I/2 on the first qubit tensored with |0⟩⟨0| on the second. An X+ test succeeds with probability 1/2."}, {"title": "Decoherence and the Classical Record — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Decoherence and the Classical Record", "url": "02-decoherence.html#exercise-10", "text": "State precisely what decoherence establishes in this model. It multiplies reduced off-diagonal entries by environmental overlap and explains lost local visibility. It does not turn the global pure state into one uniquely selected product term."}, {"title": "Losing access to a phase", "chapter": "Decoherence and the Classical Record", "url": "02-decoherence.html#section-2-1", "text": "2.1 Losing access to a phase 2.1.1 Trace out the environmental record Suppose the laboratory has produced \\(\\alpha|0,A_0\\rangle+\\beta|1,A_1\\rangle\\). Now the environment also responds to the record. Its initial state evolves to \\(|E_0\\rangle\\) or \\(|E_1\\rangle\\), giving \\(|\\Psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0,A_0,E_0\\rangle+\\beta|1,A_1,E_1\\rangle\\). Nothing in this expression has been randomly discarded. Trace out the environment and define \\(\\gamma=\\langle E_1|E_0\\rangle\\). In the correlated system–pointer basis, \\[\\rho_{SA}=\\begin{pmatrix}|\\alpha|^2&\\alpha\\beta^*\\gamma\\\\\\alpha^*\\beta\\gamma^*&|\\beta|^2\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Every lost interference term has a visible mathematical cause: the overlap of the environmental records. Identical records leave |γ|=1; orthogonal records give γ=0. For N independent environment fragments with overlap g, the combined overlap is \\(g^N\\). Even moderately distinguishable records can suppress the off-diagonal entries rapidly. Independence here is a model assumption. Correlated fragments need not obey this product law. Nor is a small overlap a proof that the environment is fundamentally irreversible. We have shown that the phase becomes inaccessible to operations confined to the chosen subsystem. The environment acquires an orthogonal record. All three qubits together remain in a coherent state; tracing out the environment removes coherence from the system–pointer description. Worked check 2.1. If g=0.9 and N=100, estimate residual overlap. Reasoned solution It is 0.9¹⁰⁰≈2.656×10⁻⁵. This is strong suppression in the independent-fragment model, not exactly zero."}, {"title": "Why some records persist", "chapter": "Decoherence and the Classical Record", "url": "02-decoherence.html#section-2-2", "text": "2.2 Why some records persist 2.2.1 The interaction selects stable alternatives Why do tables have stable positions rather than stable superpositions of distant locations? A full answer depends on their interaction with the surroundings. In a simple monitoring model take \\(H_{\\rm int}=Z\\otimes B\\). The eigenstates of Z correlate with the environment without being mixed by this interaction. A superposition of them becomes entangled. The interaction therefore distinguishes a preferred set of candidate records. This is not the claim that every environment selects the same basis. If the interaction instead monitors X, its stable alternatives differ. A system's own Hamiltonian may also compete with monitoring, so a realistic pointer basis can be approximate and time dependent. Robustness must be demonstrated with the actual dynamics. \\[\\rho_{01}(t)=e^{-\\Gamma t}\\rho_{01}(0)\\] is a useful Markovian dephasing model for constant Γ≥0. It leaves Z probabilities fixed while damping X and Y expectations. It specifies an ensemble channel, not a unique microscopic history. Many environmental models can realize it, and many stochastic descriptions can generate the same ensemble equation. The conceptual gain is substantial: stable records and suppressed interference can be explained dynamically. The remaining question is how to understand the outcomes represented by those records. Decoherence contributes to several interpretations precisely because that further question is still available. Worked check 2.2. At Γt=ln2, what happens to an initial |+⟩ state? Reasoned solution Its Bloch X coordinate falls from one to one half. The density eigenvalues become 3/4 and 1/4, so its purity is 5/8."}, {"title": "Mixtures and reversibility", "chapter": "Decoherence and the Classical Record", "url": "02-decoherence.html#section-2-3", "text": "2.3 Mixtures and reversibility 2.3.1 Distinguish local equivalence from global identity Consider the Bell state \\(|\\Phi^+\\rangle=(|00\\rangle+|11\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\) and the mixture \\(\\rho_{\\rm mix}=(|00\\rangle\\langle00|+|11\\rangle\\langle11|)/2\\). Each single qubit has density matrix I/2 in both cases. No local test on only one qubit distinguishes them. Yet the joint observable X⊗X has expectation one in the Bell state and zero in the mixture. The same subsystem density operator can therefore arise from entanglement or from an ensemble of preparations. “Improper mixture” names the first origin; “proper mixture” often names the second, relative to an account of how the ensemble was prepared. The distinction concerns the global account, not an extra local observable omitted by the density operator. In the two-qubit premeasurement, applying the same CNOT again restores |+⟩|0⟩. If a genuinely nonselective collapse has removed the joint coherence, this reversal instead leaves a 50–50 mixture of |0⟩|0⟩ and |1⟩|0⟩. An X test distinguishes the cases. Reversing an entire macroscopic environment is extraordinarily demanding, but difficulty of control and fundamental dynamical impossibility are different claims. This distinction will return in Wigner's friend. Before assigning a conditional state, always ask which degrees of freedom the proposed future experiment can coherently access. Worked check 2.3. Find a joint measurement that distinguishes Bell coherence from the mixture. Reasoned solution Measure X on each qubit and multiply the outcomes. The Bell state always has product +1; the mixture has products ±1 with equal probability."}, {"title": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts", "url": "03-bell-and-contextuality.html#exercise-1", "text": "What role does measurement independence play in the average? A common μ permits combining four correlations into one bounded integrand. Setting-dependent distributions invalidate that step."}, {"title": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts", "url": "03-bell-and-contextuality.html#exercise-2", "text": "Prove |u+v|+|u−v|≤2 for u,v∈[−1,1]. The expression equals 2 max(|u|,|v|), which is at most two."}, {"title": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts", "url": "03-bell-and-contextuality.html#exercise-3", "text": "Must Bell-local responses be deterministic? No. The bound holds for all means in [−1,1], including stochastic response functions."}, {"title": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts", "url": "03-bell-and-contextuality.html#exercise-4", "text": "Compute E₁₁ for the specified Bell experiment. The expectation of X⊗(Z−X)/√2 is −1/√2."}, {"title": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts", "url": "03-bell-and-contextuality.html#exercise-5", "text": "For v=0.8 calculate S. S≈2.2627, above two for these ideal measurements."}, {"title": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts", "url": "03-bell-and-contextuality.html#exercise-6", "text": "Why does postselecting on Bob’s outcome not enable signalling? Conditional ensembles can differ, but Alice must receive Bob’s outcome information to select them. Her unconditioned statistics do not depend on his setting."}, {"title": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts", "url": "03-bell-and-contextuality.html#exercise-7", "text": "Verify the third row product of the square. (X⊗Z)(Z⊗X)=Y⊗Y, whose square is I, so the row product is +I."}, {"title": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts", "url": "03-bell-and-contextuality.html#exercise-8", "text": "Why is assigning a new value in each context an escape from the contradiction? The same observable no longer supplies the same variable twice. The squared-value cancellation fails; this is precisely contextuality."}, {"title": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts", "url": "03-bell-and-contextuality.html#exercise-9", "text": "Does the square establish a contradiction for a single qubit? No. The construction uses two qubits, dimension four. Its nine observables cannot be read as a single-qubit value table."}, {"title": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts", "url": "03-bell-and-contextuality.html#exercise-10", "text": "Contrast Bell locality and generalized noncontextuality. Bell locality constrains spatial conditional probabilities given λ. Generalized noncontextuality requires identical representations for operationally equivalent procedures. Their assumptions and experimental structures differ."}, {"title": "A precise locality condition", "chapter": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts", "url": "03-bell-and-contextuality.html#section-3-1", "text": "3.1 A precise locality condition 3.1.1 Derive the CHSH bound Alice chooses x∈{0,1}, Bob chooses y∈{0,1}, and each records ±1. Introduce a candidate complete variable λ. Bell-local factorization says \\(p(a,b|x,y,\\lambda)=p(a|x,\\lambda)p(b|y,\\lambda)\\). Measurement independence says the distribution μ(λ) does not depend on x or y. Neither assumption should disappear behind the word “realism.” Let Aₓ(λ) and Bᵧ(λ) be the conditional mean outcomes, each between −1 and 1. For a fixed λ the CHSH expression is \\(A_0(B_0+B_1)+A_1(B_0-B_1)\\). Its magnitude is at most \\(|B_0+B_1|+|B_0-B_1|\\le2\\). Averaging over a setting-independent μ preserves the bound: \\[|S|=|E_{00}+E_{01}+E_{10}-E_{11}|\\le2.\\] Notice that we did not assume deterministic outcomes. They appear only as extreme cases of conditional means. The result constrains a combination of probabilistic and causal assumptions. Rejecting factorization is one response; rejecting measurement independence is another, with its own explanatory responsibilities. An inequality alone does not decide which account to adopt. Worked check 3.1. For deterministic B₀=B₁=−1, what is the fixed-λ CHSH value? Reasoned solution It is −2A₀, with magnitude two; the A₁ contribution vanishes."}, {"title": "Quantum correlations without messages", "chapter": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts", "url": "03-bell-and-contextuality.html#section-3-2", "text": "3.2 Quantum correlations without messages 3.2.1 Separate factorization from no-signalling Now use \\(|\\Phi^+\\rangle\\), Alice's A₀=Z, A₁=X, and Bob's \\(B_0=(Z+X)/\\sqrt2\\), \\(B_1=(Z-X)/\\sqrt2\\). The nonzero correlations XX and ZZ both equal one; XZ and ZX vanish. Substituting gives E₀₀=E₀₁=E₁₀=1/√2 and E₁₁=−1/√2, hence S=2√2. Does Bob send a message by choosing his setting? No. For a complete local POVM \\(\\{F_b^y\\}\\), \\[\\sum_b p(a,b|x,y)=\\operatorname{Tr}\\!\\left[\\rho(E_a^x\\otimes\\sum_bF_b^y)\\right]=\\operatorname{Tr}[\\rho(E_a^x\\otimes I)].\\] Alice's marginal is independent of y. This is operational no-signalling. It does not imply the stronger factorization conditional on λ. Confusing the two makes it impossible to describe theories that reproduce Bell correlations without controllable faster-than-light communication. The calculation is ideal. Experimental inference also needs appropriate treatment of detection, setting choice, timing, and statistical uncertainty. Here we derive the theoretical benchmark rather than claiming a particular experiment closes every loophole. Later chapters will use this benchmark to ask what a proposed ontology must reproduce. Worked check 3.2. For white-noise visibility v, ρ=v|Φ+⟩⟨Φ+|+(1−v)I/4, when is CHSH violated with these settings? Reasoned solution All correlations scale by v, so S=2√2v. A violation requires v>1/√2."}, {"title": "Contextual values", "chapter": "Bell, Contextuality, and the Limits of Classical Accounts", "url": "03-bell-and-contextuality.html#section-3-3", "text": "3.3 Contextual values 3.3.1 An algebraic obstruction to a global value table Contextuality asks a different question: could every observable have a fixed value independent of which compatible observables accompany it? For sharp measurements consider the array \\[\\begin{pmatrix}X\\otimes I&I\\otimes X&X\\otimes X\\\\I\\otimes Z&Z\\otimes I&Z\\otimes Z\\\\X\\otimes Z&Z\\otimes X&Y\\otimes Y\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Each row and column consists of commuting operators. The three row products and first two column products are +I; the final column product is −I. Suppose each entry has a context-independent value ±1 and the value of a commuting product equals the product of values. Multiplying all six required constraints gives +1 on the left, because every entry occurs twice, but −1 on the right. No such assignment exists. This is a state-independent contradiction for that value model in dimension four. It does not forbid all descriptions containing additional variables: those descriptions can be contextual. It also is not the complete modern definition of contextuality. Generalized noncontextuality concerns operationally equivalent preparations, transformations, and measurements and does not simply assume predetermined values for every effect. The lesson is methodological. Identify the compatibility relations and the value rule before invoking a no-go theorem. An interpretation earns its place by explaining how it handles the assumptions, not by declaring the theorem irrelevant. Worked check 3.3. Verify the last column product. Reasoned solution (X⊗X)(Z⊗Z)=−Y⊗Y because XZ=−iY. Multiplying by Y⊗Y gives −I."}, {"title": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories", "url": "04-operational-and-histories.html#exercise-1", "text": "Compare p(Z=0 then X=+) with p(X=+ then Z=0) for |0⟩. The first is 1×1/2=1/2. The second is 1/2×1/2=1/4. Measurement order changes the experiment."}, {"title": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories", "url": "04-operational-and-histories.html#exercise-2", "text": "Does omitting a detector outcome from a report restore interference? No. Forgetting corresponds to a nonselective instrument. Restoration requires erasing or reversing the physical distinguishing record coherently."}, {"title": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories", "url": "04-operational-and-histories.html#exercise-3", "text": "Why avoid using Copenhagen as an exact equation set? The name covers distinct views about classical descriptions and reduction. State the particular prescription or commitments being analyzed."}, {"title": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories", "url": "04-operational-and-histories.html#exercise-4", "text": "Show that a single-time projective history family is consistent. For a≠b, Tr(PaρPb)=Tr(PbPaρ)=0. Thus its off-diagonal decoherence-functional entries vanish."}, {"title": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories", "url": "04-operational-and-histories.html#exercise-5", "text": "Derive the interference term for a union of two histories. The probability is Dαα+Dββ+Dαβ+Dβα, hence the two diagonals plus 2 Re Dαβ."}, {"title": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories", "url": "04-operational-and-histories.html#exercise-6", "text": "Is medium decoherence necessary for just one pairwise sum rule? No. For a given pair, a vanishing real part suffices for additivity. We use the stronger condition to specify a convenient consistent framework."}, {"title": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories", "url": "04-operational-and-histories.html#exercise-7", "text": "Compute D for the two histories ending in X=+. Each vector is |+⟩/2, so the cross term is 1/4 and this family fails medium decoherence."}, {"title": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories", "url": "04-operational-and-histories.html#exercise-8", "text": "What changes when orthogonal records are appended? The cross term gains a factor ⟨A1|A0⟩=0. The enlarged experiment can admit additive probabilities for the recorded alternatives."}, {"title": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories", "url": "04-operational-and-histories.html#exercise-9", "text": "Can two separately consistent frameworks always be combined? No. The single-framework rule forbids combining incompatible families. Separate internal consistency does not establish a common probability space."}, {"title": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories", "url": "04-operational-and-histories.html#exercise-10", "text": "Propose a fair research question about framework selection. Given a decohering apparatus model, determine which families track stable records, then ask whether stability singles out a unique family or only an approximate class of useful descriptions."}, {"title": "What a measurement prescription supplies", "chapter": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories", "url": "04-operational-and-histories.html#section-4-1", "text": "4.1 What a measurement prescription supplies 4.1.1 Conditioning within a specified experimental context One disciplined response to the measurement problem is to begin with experimental procedures and their probabilities. State preparation, measurement setting, and recorded outcome are treated as the terms in which a theory makes predictions. The quantum state then organizes expectations about those procedures. This is an operational stance; by itself it need not supply a microscopic ontology for every detector. The label “Copenhagen” covers several historical views, including different emphases on classical descriptions, complementarity, and state reduction. It should not be treated as one precisely specified modern model. In particular, saying that measurement requires consciousness is not a mathematical consequence of the standard measurement prescription. For an ideal sharp measurement with projector Pₐ, the Lüders instrument gives \\(\\mathcal I_a(\\rho)=P_a\\rho P_a\\). A subsequent sharp measurement Qᵦ has joint probability \\[p(a,b)=\\operatorname{Tr}(Q_bP_a\\rho P_aQ_b).\\] The order and the actual procedure matter. Starting from |+⟩, an unread Z measurement followed by X gives equal X outcomes. An X measurement alone gives + with certainty. The unread record still corresponds to an interaction that changed the relevant state. Dropping its label does not undo the intervention. Worked check 4.1. After an unread Z measurement on |+⟩, what is p(X=+)? Reasoned solution The state is I/2, so p=1/2. Without the Z intervention it would be one."}, {"title": "Histories as quantum alternatives", "chapter": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories", "url": "04-operational-and-histories.html#section-4-2", "text": "4.2 Histories as quantum alternatives 4.2.1 Compute the decoherence functional Consistent histories asks when sequences of quantum properties can be assigned ordinary joint probabilities without treating measurement as fundamental. In a finite closed system let \\(\\{P_{a_j}(t_j)\\}\\) be exhaustive orthogonal projectors in the Heisenberg picture at each time. A history α=(a₁,…,aₙ) has class operator \\(C_\\alpha=P_{a_n}(t_n)\\cdots P_{a_1}(t_1)\\). \\[D(\\alpha,\\beta)=\\operatorname{Tr}(C_\\alpha\\rho C_\\beta^\\dagger),\\qquad p(\\alpha)=D(\\alpha,\\alpha).\\] The diagonal entries are nonnegative, but that alone is not enough. On coarse-graining two alternatives, the candidate probability includes \\(2\\operatorname{Re}D(\\alpha,\\beta)\\). Classical additivity requires that contribution to vanish. We use the sufficient “medium decoherence” condition D(α,β)=0 for distinct histories; weaker consistency conditions can be formulated, so our convention must be stated. A history family satisfying this condition supports an ordinary probability calculus internally. This gives a precise technical role to mutually exclusive alternatives extending across time. The condition is a property of a family, its initial state, and its dynamics; it is not guaranteed merely because each projector is individually well defined. Worked check 4.2. Why are diagonal decoherence-functional entries nonnegative? Reasoned solution Write them as Tr[(Cα√ρ)(Cα√ρ)†], a squared Hilbert–Schmidt norm."}, {"title": "One framework at a time", "chapter": "Operational Accounts and Consistent Histories", "url": "04-operational-and-histories.html#section-4-3", "text": "4.3 One framework at a time 4.3.1 Find exactly where classical addition fails Try a small family. Set the Hamiltonian to zero and start in |+⟩. Ask for Z=0 or Z=1 at the first time and X=+ or X=− at the second. The two branch vectors ending in X=+ are \\[Q_+P_0|+\\rangle=\\tfrac12|+\\rangle,\\qquad Q_+P_1|+\\rangle=\\tfrac12|+\\rangle.\\] Their overlap is 1/4, not zero. Summing their individual squared norms gives 1/2, but the squared norm of their sum is one. The unmeasured intermediate alternatives interfere. You may not regard them as mutually exclusive classical paths while retaining the final interference prediction. If an apparatus actually records Z in orthogonal states, those branch vectors acquire orthogonal record factors and the corresponding cross term vanishes in the enlarged description. That is a different physical experiment. Consistent histories also permits different incompatible consistent families, but its single-framework rule disallows combining their propositions into one classical argument. This approach supplies a formal discipline for quantum reasoning. A fair critique asks how frameworks are chosen for a question and how their status relates to physical reality. It should not replace that discussion with the mistaken claim that every imaginable history already possesses an additive probability. Worked check 4.3. Compute the two branch vectors ending in X=−. Reasoned solution They are +|−⟩/2 and −|−⟩/2. Their sum is zero, but their individual probabilities would sum to 1/2; the cross terms cancel that sum."}, {"title": "Everett and Relative States — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Everett and Relative States", "url": "05-everett.html#exercise-1", "text": "Is a relative-state projection necessarily a new physical collapse? No. In the relative-state account the projection defines a conditional state while the universal vector is retained."}, {"title": "Everett and Relative States — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Everett and Relative States", "url": "05-everett.html#exercise-2", "text": "What happens when the conditioning record has zero amplitude? The normalized relative state is undefined because the conditioning event has zero norm; it is not an additional realized branch."}, {"title": "Everett and Relative States — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Everett and Relative States", "url": "05-everett.html#exercise-3", "text": "What extra interpretive claim distinguishes Everett from ordinary conditional-state calculations? Everett retains universal unitary evolution and treats the full state as describing the physical situation, including observer correlations."}, {"title": "Everett and Relative States — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Everett and Relative States", "url": "05-everett.html#exercise-4", "text": "Write |Φ+⟩ in the X basis. It equals (|++⟩+|−−⟩)/√2. This demonstrates that a basis expansion alone does not identify a classical record basis."}, {"title": "Everett and Relative States — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Everett and Relative States", "url": "05-everett.html#exercise-5", "text": "Why is environmental refinement a problem for branch counting? Counting changes its relative frequency of terms although its total norm and macro-observable predictions are unchanged."}, {"title": "Everett and Relative States — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Everett and Relative States", "url": "05-everett.html#exercise-6", "text": "Is decoherence an exact count of worlds? No. It supplies dynamical criteria for robust approximately independent records, generally without a unique exact branch count."}, {"title": "Everett and Relative States — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Everett and Relative States", "url": "05-everett.html#exercise-7", "text": "Prove additivity of squared norm for orthogonal vectors. The cross terms ⟨u|v⟩ and its conjugate vanish, leaving ||u||²+||v||²."}, {"title": "Everett and Relative States — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Everett and Relative States", "url": "05-everett.html#exercise-8", "text": "For N=2 and p=1/4 find weights of k=0,1,2. They are 9/16, 6/16, and 1/16. The two one-success sequences contribute separately to the middle weight."}, {"title": "Everett and Relative States — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Everett and Relative States", "url": "05-everett.html#exercise-9", "text": "Does binomial concentration alone solve the probability problem? No. It establishes concentration with respect to Born weight; the interpretive justification for using that weight remains an assumption or further argument."}, {"title": "Everett and Relative States — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Everett and Relative States", "url": "05-everett.html#exercise-10", "text": "How should a decision-theoretic Born-rule claim be evaluated? State the allowed acts, preference axioms, continuity or equivalence assumptions, and conclusion. Then examine whether those assumptions are justified for observers in a branching state."}, {"title": "Relative states", "chapter": "Everett and Relative States", "url": "05-everett.html#section-5-1", "text": "5.1 Relative states 5.1.1 Condition a description without deleting the global state Return to the state \\(|\\Psi\\rangle=\\alpha|0,A_0\\rangle+\\beta|1,A_1\\rangle\\). Everett's proposal takes the universal quantum state and its unitary dynamics seriously, including the observer among the systems described. The theory does not introduce a new stochastic event that deletes one term. Instead, a system state can be specified relative to a record. \\[|\\psi_{S|A_k}\\rangle=\\frac{\\langle A_k|\\Psi\\rangle}{\\|\\langle A_k|\\Psi\\rangle\\|}.\\] For nonzero α and β the relative states are |0⟩ and |1⟩, up to irrelevant phases. In each correlated record component, the apparatus agrees with the measured system. This is how relative-state reasoning addresses the appearance of definite correlated observations. The global state remains the superposition. The formal conditional vector alone is not the whole interpretation: ordinary quantum mechanics also uses conditional states. The additional commitment is to the continued global state and an account of observers within it. Modern Everettian accounts commonly describe approximately autonomous decohered branches, rather than adding a physical splitting operation to the Hamiltonian. There is therefore an explanatory task, not merely a change of notation: why should the patterns identified as branches support the experiences and expectations of observers? Worked check 5.1. Find the relative system state for a record A₁ when β=i/2. Reasoned solution Projection gives i|1⟩/2; normalization gives i|1⟩, physically the same ray as |1⟩. Its branch weight is 1/4."}, {"title": "Branches and stable records", "chapter": "Everett and Relative States", "url": "05-everett.html#section-5-2", "text": "5.2 Branches and stable records 5.2.1 Use decoherence rather than an arbitrary basis expansion A vector can be expanded in many bases. Why privilege one expansion as a description of records? The Bell state can be written in both the Z-correlated and X-correlated forms. Algebra alone cannot select a classical narrative. The environment and the dynamics matter because they stabilize some records and rapidly disperse interference between them. Suppose |A₀⟩ and |A₁⟩ leave distinguishable records across many uncontrolled degrees of freedom. Within the observables accessible to an observer in one such record, interference with the other record becomes negligible. An approximately autonomous branch is a dynamical and coarse-grained notion. Its definition need not assign an exact integer number of worlds at every instant. \\[\\alpha|0,A_0\\rangle\\longrightarrow \\frac{\\alpha}{\\sqrt m}\\sum_{j=1}^m|0,A_0,E_j\\rangle\\] illustrates a harmless refinement of one outcome into m orthogonal environmental alternatives. Its total squared norm is still |α|². A rule that gives equal probability to every written term would change the probability of the macroscopic record merely because its environmental description was refined. Stable predictions require a measure insensitive to such arbitrary counting. Decoherence thus helps explain robust record structure, but the role of branch weights still needs an argument. We should resist treating the word “branch” as a new primitive probability unit. Worked check 5.2. Split a branch of weight 3/4 into three equal orthogonal components. Reasoned solution Each new component has weight 1/4, and their weights sum to 3/4. A macro-record probability based on total weight is unchanged."}, {"title": "Probability in a unitary world", "chapter": "Everett and Relative States", "url": "05-everett.html#section-5-3", "text": "5.3 Probability in a unitary world 5.3.1 Identify the assumptions behind a weight rule For mutually orthogonal components, squared norm is additive: \\(\\|u+v\\|^2=\\|u\\|^2+\\|v\\|^2\\). It is also invariant under unitary refinement and normalized for a normalized state. These mathematical properties explain why squared amplitudes are natural weights. They do not alone establish what an observer should expect when every decohered outcome component remains in the universal state. Everettian probability programs add assumptions about rational preferences, self-locating uncertainty, or typicality. Decision-theoretic arguments, for example, constrain how an agent values quantum acts and identify conditions under which preferences can be represented using Born weights. A theorem derived from those conditions is not a derivation from the Schrödinger equation alone. Whether its assumptions adequately explain observed probability is part of the interpretive debate. If Born weights are used for N repeated independent preparations with outcome weight p, total weight of the k-success record subspace is \\[w(k)=\\binom Nk p^k(1-p)^{N-k}.\\] The mean frequency is p and its variance is p(1−p)/N. This concentration is mathematically clear. The remaining issue is why branch weight should govern empirical expectation or typicality, rather than merely being a number attached to components. Keep that distinction visible when assessing any proposed solution. Worked check 5.3. For N=100 and p=1/4, find the standard deviation of the relative frequency. Reasoned solution It is √[p(1−p)/N]=√0.001875≈0.0433. This calculation assumes the Born-weight assignment."}, {"title": "Bohmian Mechanics — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Bohmian Mechanics", "url": "06-bohmian-mechanics.html#exercise-1", "text": "For a real stationary spatial wavefunction, calculate velocity away from nodes. Its imaginary part vanishes, so the velocity is zero. The global time-dependent phase does not change the spatial derivative."}, {"title": "Bohmian Mechanics — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Bohmian Mechanics", "url": "06-bohmian-mechanics.html#exercise-2", "text": "Why exclude nodes when writing ∇Ψ/Ψ? The expression is undefined at Ψ=0. Trajectory existence and behavior near nodes require additional mathematical care; the local formula assumes nonzero density."}, {"title": "Bohmian Mechanics — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Bohmian Mechanics", "url": "06-bohmian-mechanics.html#exercise-3", "text": "For a ring of length L with k=2πn/L, find the speed. The signed velocity is 2πℏn/(mL), with magnitude its absolute value."}, {"title": "Bohmian Mechanics — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Bohmian Mechanics", "url": "06-bohmian-mechanics.html#exercise-4", "text": "Substitute ρ=|Ψ|² into the configuration continuity equation. It becomes exactly the Schrödinger continuity equation. This establishes preservation of the equilibrium distribution under appropriate regularity conditions."}, {"title": "Bohmian Mechanics — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Bohmian Mechanics", "url": "06-bohmian-mechanics.html#exercise-5", "text": "Does equivariance prove relaxation from any initial distribution? No. Invariance says an initially matching density remains matching. Relaxation of other densities is a separate dynamical claim and is not universally guaranteed."}, {"title": "Bohmian Mechanics — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Bohmian Mechanics", "url": "06-bohmian-mechanics.html#exercise-6", "text": "Why does an equilibrium ensemble reproduce pointer frequencies? Their probabilities are integrals of |Ψ|² over those regions. For normalized separated record packets, these give the corresponding squared amplitudes."}, {"title": "Bohmian Mechanics — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Bohmian Mechanics", "url": "06-bohmian-mechanics.html#exercise-7", "text": "Is effective collapse a modification of Schrödinger evolution? No. The full state continues evolving unitarily. One packet suffices approximately for guidance while supports remain separated near the actual configuration."}, {"title": "Bohmian Mechanics — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Bohmian Mechanics", "url": "06-bohmian-mechanics.html#exercise-8", "text": "Does Bell’s theorem exclude this theory because it has hidden variables? No. The guiding dynamics is generally nonlocal for entangled states. It does not satisfy the Bell-local assumption excluded by the inequality."}, {"title": "Bohmian Mechanics — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Bohmian Mechanics", "url": "06-bohmian-mechanics.html#exercise-9", "text": "Why does a definite position not imply a predetermined value of every observable? The recorded value depends on wavefunction, configuration, and measurement arrangement. It need not reveal a context-independent property associated with the operator."}, {"title": "Bohmian Mechanics — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Bohmian Mechanics", "url": "06-bohmian-mechanics.html#exercise-10", "text": "State a limitation of the equations in this chapter. They describe nonrelativistic spinless particles without vector potentials. Spin, gauge fields, particle creation, and relativistic structure need additional formulation."}, {"title": "State and guiding law", "chapter": "Bohmian Mechanics", "url": "06-bohmian-mechanics.html#section-6-1", "text": "6.1 State and guiding law 6.1.1 Derive a velocity field from probability current Bohmian mechanics supplements the wavefunction with an actual configuration Q(t). For N nonrelativistic spinless particles without vector potentials, the wavefunction obeys the usual Schrödinger equation on configuration space. The positions obey a guiding equation. Writing the law explicitly prevents the vague image of tiny classical balls following Newtonian trajectories. \\[\\dot Q_k=\\left.\\frac{\\hbar}{m_k}\\operatorname{Im}\\frac{\\nabla_k\\Psi}{\\Psi}\\right|_{q=Q},\\qquad j_k=\\frac{\\hbar}{m_k}\\operatorname{Im}(\\Psi^*\\nabla_k\\Psi).\\] Away from nodes, the velocity is jₖ/|Ψ|². For a local polar form Ψ=R exp(iS/ℏ), it is ∇ₖS/mₖ. The actual configuration and the wavefunction jointly determine the motion. Spin and electromagnetic potentials require the appropriate generalized current; the displayed formula is deliberately restricted. For a plane wave on a periodic line, Ψ∝exp(ikx), the velocity is ℏk/m. For a nondegenerate stationary state with a real spatial wavefunction, the spatial phase gradient is zero and the velocity vanishes away from nodes. This may surprise classical intuition, but it follows directly from the stated theory. One should assess the model by its full measurement predictions rather than replacing its law with a classical guess. Worked check 6.1. For Ψ=R(x)e^{ikx} with positive real R, find v. Reasoned solution The logarithmic derivative is R′/R+ik. Only k contributes to its imaginary part, giving v=ℏk/m."}, {"title": "Quantum equilibrium", "chapter": "Bohmian Mechanics", "url": "06-bohmian-mechanics.html#section-6-2", "text": "6.2 Quantum equilibrium 6.2.1 Why the Born density is preserved Schrödinger evolution implies the continuity equation \\[\\partial_t|\\Psi|^2+\\sum_k\\nabla_k\\cdot j_k=0.\\] An ensemble of configurations transported by the guiding velocity satisfies \\(\\partial_t\\rho+\\sum_k\\nabla_k\\cdot(\\rho v_k)=0\\). Because jₖ=|Ψ|²vₖ, the density |Ψ|² obeys the same transport equation. Subject to the usual regularity and boundary conditions, if ρ=|Ψ|² initially, this equality is preserved. This property is called equivariance. Equivariance does not say that every arbitrary initial distribution magically becomes |Ψ|². Quantum equilibrium is an additional statistical condition; typicality accounts explain its role by considering suitable measures on configurations, while relaxation accounts investigate particular nonequilibrium dynamics. These arguments should not be conflated with the continuity-equation identity. In equilibrium, ensembles reproduce the familiar position probabilities and, with a suitable analysis of measurement devices, the usual quantum statistics. The Born density has a precise dynamical role here: it is preserved by the combined wave and configuration dynamics. Its interpretation and justification are still distinct questions from preservation. Worked check 6.2. Does equivariance show that ρ=2|Ψ|² is a distinct equilibrium density? Reasoned solution No. It is not normalized when Ψ is normalized. Equivariance concerns evolution of the normalized |Ψ|² density; arbitrary alternatives require their own initial conditions."}, {"title": "Measurement and nonlocality", "chapter": "Bohmian Mechanics", "url": "06-bohmian-mechanics.html#section-6-3", "text": "6.3 Measurement and nonlocality 6.3.1 Follow the actual configuration into a record Let an apparatus interaction produce two macroscopically disjoint packets in configuration space, Ψ=αΨ₀+βΨ₁. The actual configuration lies in one packet's support. When the other packet has negligible support near that configuration, the guiding equation is effectively governed by the occupied packet. This is effective collapse. It is not a literal deletion of the other wave component. If the packets later overlap coherently, the neglected component can affect motion again. The effective wavefunction is therefore justified by dynamical separation, not by an irrevocable declaration that unused terms are nonexistent. This will matter for hypothetical measurements on entire laboratories. For entangled Ψ, the velocity of one particle generally depends on the whole configuration, including distant coordinates. Bohmian mechanics is nonlocal in this sense. In equilibrium it nevertheless reproduces operational no-signalling. A relativistic extension is a further task; the nonrelativistic equation here is not already a Lorentz-covariant completion. Finally, an operator measurement can depend on the full experimental arrangement. The theory does not assign every Hermitian operator a context-independent intrinsic value. Definite positions therefore do not evade the contextuality theorem by pretending its impossible value table exists. Worked check 6.3. Why can an empty packet matter after recombination? Reasoned solution The velocity depends on the full wavefunction and its gradient at the actual configuration. Overlapping packets contribute interference terms there even if only one was occupied while they were separated."}, {"title": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives", "url": "07-collapse-models.html#exercise-1", "text": "Why is the jump-center expression a probability density rather than one probability? The probability in a region is the integral of ||L(x)Ψ||² over that region. The completeness integral ensures total probability one."}, {"title": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives", "url": "07-collapse-models.html#exercise-2", "text": "Verify the one-dimensional Gaussian completeness normalization. The squared prefactor is 1/(√π r), and the Gaussian integral is √π r, giving one for each q."}, {"title": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives", "url": "07-collapse-models.html#exercise-3", "text": "Does a Gaussian jump eliminate all distant amplitude exactly? No. The multiplier is nonzero at finite separation. Suppression can be very strong without compact support or exact elimination."}, {"title": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives", "url": "07-collapse-models.html#exercise-4", "text": "For a Poisson process with total rate Nλ, find the mean waiting time. The mean is 1/(Nλ), assuming independent constant-rate constituent events."}, {"title": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives", "url": "07-collapse-models.html#exercise-5", "text": "Why might a jump fail to distinguish two branches? If their positions nearly coincide on the localization scale, the Gaussian factors are similar. The event gives little relative suppression."}, {"title": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives", "url": "07-collapse-models.html#exercise-6", "text": "Show trace preservation of the ensemble jump term. The integrated gain has trace Tr[ρ∫L†L]=Trρ, which cancels the loss term for each particle."}, {"title": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives", "url": "07-collapse-models.html#exercise-7", "text": "Does observed decoherence uniquely identify objective collapse? No. Environmental entanglement can produce the same reduced coherence loss. Discriminating tests need calibrated alternatives and model-specific signatures."}, {"title": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives", "url": "07-collapse-models.html#exercise-8", "text": "What does a negative upper bound in the visibility toy model mean? The stated inputs cannot all fit that model with a nonnegative extra rate. Check calibration, uncertainties, and environmental assumptions."}, {"title": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives", "url": "07-collapse-models.html#exercise-9", "text": "Why can’t a bound on Γ_new be directly called a bound on λ? The effective visibility rate depends on geometry, particle content, separation, and r_C as well as λ. That relation must be derived for the selected collapse model."}, {"title": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives", "url": "07-collapse-models.html#exercise-10", "text": "Design a minimal report for a hypothetical collapse test. Specify preparation, mass and geometry, separation and time, environmental budget, measured observable and confidence procedure, collapse equation and parameter convention, and the resulting conditional parameter region."}, {"title": "Changing the dynamics", "chapter": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives", "url": "07-collapse-models.html#section-7-1", "text": "7.1 Changing the dynamics 7.1.1 Specify a jump law and its probability density Objective-collapse models change quantum dynamics so that macroscopic alternatives do not indefinitely coexist as one uncollapsed superposition. They are therefore more than different verbal readings of an unchanged formalism. A complete model must specify when localization occurs, how the state changes, and how outcomes are distributed. In a simple GRW-type position-jump model for distinguishable particles, each particle undergoes localization events at rate λ. A jump for particle i with center x acts through \\[L_i(x)=(\\pi r_C^2)^{-3/4}\\exp[-(\\hat q_i-x)^2/(2r_C^2)],\\qquad \\int d^3x\\,L_i(x)^\\dagger L_i(x)=I.\\] The center has probability density \\(\\|L_i(x)\\Psi\\|^2\\), and the updated vector is \\(L_i(x)\\Psi/\\|L_i(x)\\Psi\\|\\). Between jumps the state evolves with the Hamiltonian. Here r_C fixes the Gaussian convention; other texts can parameterize the same width differently. The jump multiplies the configuration wavefunction by a localized Gaussian. It narrows spatial alternatives probabilistically, rather than asking an observer to declare a measurement. Gaussian tails remain nonzero, however, so the interpretation of macroscopic definiteness and the choice of material ontology still need attention. A wavefunction law alone is not a complete description of flashes or mass density. Worked check 7.1. For Nλt=3, find the probability of at least one jump. Reasoned solution It is 1−e⁻³≈0.9502. This is an event probability, not a universal branch-resolution probability."}, {"title": "Amplification and ensemble evolution", "chapter": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives", "url": "07-collapse-models.html#section-7-2", "text": "7.2 Amplification and ensemble evolution 7.2.1 Separate trajectories from their average If N distinguishable constituents each have independent rate λ, the probability of no event over time t is exp(−Nλt). For a rigid superposition whose corresponding particle locations are separated far beyond r_C, one localization can strongly suppress one branch relative to the other. This is the basic amplification mechanism. The formula does not claim that every event resolves every possible pair of states: separations and geometry matter. Averaging the jump process gives an ensemble master equation of the form \\[\\dot\\rho=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,\\rho]+\\lambda\\sum_i\\left(\\int d^3x\\,L_i(x)\\rho L_i(x)^\\dagger-\\rho\\right).\\] The jump law supplies more information than this average. To see why, consider the toy qubit equation \\(\\dot\\rho=\\kappa(Z\\rho Z-\\rho)\\). Its off-diagonal entries decay at rate 2κ. Random unitary Z flips generate this same equation, yet an individual |+⟩ trajectory then merely switches between |+⟩ and |−⟩. It does not localize into a Z eigenstate. Thus decay of an ensemble coherence is not enough to establish collapse of individual states. The state-vector stochastic process, its ontology, and the ensemble predictions must be specified separately. The qubit example is a warning about inference, not a replacement for the spatial collapse model. Worked check 7.2. Solve the toy master equation for ρ₀₁. Reasoned solution Since (ZρZ)₀₁=−ρ₀₁, its derivative is −2κρ₀₁ and ρ₀₁(t)=e⁻²κᵗρ₀₁(0)."}, {"title": "A testable proposal", "chapter": "Objective Collapse and Experimental Alternatives", "url": "07-collapse-models.html#section-7-3", "text": "7.3 A testable proposal 7.3.1 Distinguish new dynamics from ordinary environmental noise A modified theory can be investigated experimentally when its additional dynamics predicts observable differences. In collapse models, suppressed interference, momentum diffusion, or heating can provide signatures, depending on the model and coupling. Ordinary gas collisions, radiation, vibration, and detector noise can also suppress interference. A credible test must compare quantitative models rather than treating any missing visibility as evidence for collapse. For an explicitly simplified visibility model, write \\(V(t)=V_0\\exp[-(\\Gamma_{\\rm env}+\\Gamma_{\\rm new})t]\\). If environmental decay is independently known and the measured visibility has a lower bound V_min, the allowed extra rate satisfies \\[\\Gamma_{\\rm new}\\le-\\frac1t\\log(V_{\\min}/V_0)-\\Gamma_{\\rm env}.\\] This bound is conditional on the exponential model, calibrated visibility, and uncertainty treatment. A negative right side signals incompatibility of those inputs with a nonnegative additional rate; it is not a measured negative collapse rate. Real constraints on λ and r_C require the model-specific relationship between localization parameters and the measured signal. The research habit here is transferable: publish assumptions, units, nuisance parameters, and statistical confidence. Distinguish a bound on one parameterized collapse model from a verdict about all interpretations. No current experimental limit is asserted by our hypothetical examples. Worked check 7.3. With V₀=1, V_min=0.9, t=1 second, and Γ_env=0.05 s⁻¹, bound Γ_new. Reasoned solution The hypothetical bound is −ln(0.9)−0.05≈0.05536 s⁻¹, assuming the stated environmental estimate and visibility model."}, {"title": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism", "url": "08-relational-and-qbism.html#exercise-1", "text": "Does relational observer mean conscious mind? No. A system participating in an interaction can serve as a relational reference; consciousness is not required by that usage."}, {"title": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism", "url": "08-relational-and-qbism.html#exercise-2", "text": "Write the outside state after an ideal friend measurement of |+⟩. It is (|0,F₀⟩+|1,F₁⟩)/√2 if the full laboratory remains coherent."}, {"title": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism", "url": "08-relational-and-qbism.html#exercise-3", "text": "Why is reading the friend’s record different from measuring interference of the laboratory? The record measurement distinguishes |0,F₀⟩ and |1,F₁⟩. An interference measurement projects onto their superpositions; it is an incompatible procedure."}, {"title": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism", "url": "08-relational-and-qbism.html#exercise-4", "text": "Find p(X+) and p(Z+) for r=(0.6,0,0.8). The state is pure because |r|=1. The probabilities are 0.8 and 0.9."}, {"title": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism", "url": "08-relational-and-qbism.html#exercise-5", "text": "If p(Z+)=1, what is p(X+) for a valid qubit state? r_z=1 forces r_x=r_y=0, so p(X+)=1/2."}, {"title": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism", "url": "08-relational-and-qbism.html#exercise-6", "text": "Why is QBism not merely unrestricted subjective guessing? Probabilities must satisfy coherence conditions and quantum constraints such as positivity and the Born rule linking different actions."}, {"title": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism", "url": "08-relational-and-qbism.html#exercise-7", "text": "Give one important distinction between QBism and relational quantum mechanics. QBism centers an agent’s actions, experiences, and personal probabilities; relational mechanics centers physical events relative to interacting systems."}, {"title": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism", "url": "08-relational-and-qbism.html#exercise-8", "text": "Does personal probability automatically imply a ψ-epistemic ontological model? No. A ψ-epistemic ontological model presupposes an underlying state space and overlapping preparation distributions. QBism need not adopt that model structure."}, {"title": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism", "url": "08-relational-and-qbism.html#exercise-9", "text": "What can the ideal agreement calculation establish? It establishes zero probability of mismatched records for the specified comparison. It does not by itself establish an observer-independent global event ontology."}, {"title": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism", "url": "08-relational-and-qbism.html#exercise-10", "text": "How should a claim that an interpretation is local be assessed? Ask whether it means no controllable signalling, Bell-local factorization, or a different account of physical events. The first does not imply the second, and CHSH statistics remain unchanged."}, {"title": "Relational events", "chapter": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism", "url": "08-relational-and-qbism.html#section-8-1", "text": "8.1 Relational events 8.1.1 Make the reference system explicit A friend interacts with a qubit and records zero or one. An outside system can still use \\((|0,F_0\\rangle+|1,F_1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\) for the combined laboratory if the interaction remains coherent. Relational quantum mechanics treats values and events as relative to interacting systems, rather than demanding one observer-independent quantum state that simultaneously describes all facts in the same way. “Observer” here need not mean a conscious person. A physical system can participate in an interaction relative to which another system has a value. That differs from a claim that minds create matter. The conceptual work lies in specifying relations between events and in explaining comparisons of records, not in assigning a privileged mental role to a detector. If the outside system later reads the friend's Z record, the interaction correlates their records. Conditional on the friend's zero record, an ideal subsequent Z readout agrees with it. If instead the outside system coherently measures an incompatible observable of the whole laboratory, the experiment is different. The formal state calculation does not by itself settle the philosophical status of relative facts. It does show why apparently inconsistent descriptions should be indexed by the systems and interactions involved before being combined. “Relative” must constrain an account, not merely excuse any contradiction. Worked check 8.1. If W reads the friend’s Z record ideally, what is the probability of mismatching records? Reasoned solution Zero in the ideal model: the joint state contains only matching |F₀,W₀⟩ and |F₁,W₁⟩ components."}, {"title": "Agents and probability assignments", "chapter": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism", "url": "08-relational-and-qbism.html#section-8-2", "text": "8.2 Agents and probability assignments 8.2.1 Use the Born rule as a normative constraint QBism assigns a different role to the quantum state: it expresses an agent's personal probabilities for the consequences of that agent's actions on the world. Even a probability-one assignment is a commitment in the agent's expectations, not automatically an assertion that the outcome was a pre-existing property. The Born rule imposes a specifically quantum constraint on those commitments. For a qubit, an agent's state can be written \\(\\rho=(I+\\mathbf r\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\sigma)/2\\), with |r|≤1. If the intended measurement is spin along a unit direction n, \\[p(+|\\mathbf n)=\\frac{1+\\mathbf r\\cdot\\mathbf n}{2}.\\] These probabilities are not arbitrary. Positivity and the Born rule relate expectations for different possible actions. For example, assigning probability one to both X+ and Z+ would require rₓ=r_z=1, violating |r|≤1. Personal probability does not mean any collection of numbers is acceptable. This perspective treats an outcome as an experience for an agent and an action as an engagement with an external world. It is not simply a hidden-variable model in which a state represents ignorance about an underlying λ. Its distinctive explanatory commitments should be assessed on their own terms, including how it accounts for intersubjective scientific practice. Worked check 8.2. Can a valid qubit state assign p(X+)=p(Z+)=0.9? Reasoned solution Those assignments require rₓ=r_z=0.8, so |r|²≥1.28>1. No positive qubit density operator satisfies both."}, {"title": "Agreement and comparison", "chapter": "Relational Quantum Mechanics and QBism", "url": "08-relational-and-qbism.html#section-8-3", "text": "8.3 Agreement and comparison 8.3.1 Do not exchange states without exchanging their conditions Both relational and QBist approaches resist treating every state assignment as an observer-independent physical wave. That common feature should not erase their differences. Relational mechanics concerns physical interactions and relative events between systems. QBism places agents, actions, and personal probability at the center of its account. An inanimate subsystem as relational observer is not automatically a QBist agent. Imagine two people who assign |0⟩ and |+⟩ to a qubit. Their Z predictions are one and one half. This is not an algebraic contradiction: the assignments may reflect different preparation information or different conditions. If they claim identical preparation information and agree on the same operational procedure, however, their differing assignments become a substantive question to resolve. A record comparison is itself a physical interaction. For an ideal correlated state \\(\\sum_k c_k|k,F_k\\rangle\\), an external Z readout can append |Wₖ⟩, giving \\(\\sum_k c_k|k,F_k,W_k\\rangle\\). There is no term with mismatched F and W records. This calculation establishes agreement for that readout, while leaving the account of global or relative actuality to the interpretation. Be equally careful about locality claims. A reinterpretation of what probabilities mean does not change the numerical CHSH value. Distinguish a philosophical account of locality from the precise factorization condition proved impossible for the stated ontological model. Worked check 8.3. Can different quantum-state assignments be compared without specifying the preparation information? Reasoned solution Not reliably. First identify whose probabilities or relations are involved, what information is conditioned on, and which future action is being predicted."}, {"title": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument", "url": "09-quantum-state-ontology.html#exercise-1", "text": "Explain the difference between ψ-ontic and ψ-complete. ψ-ontic means distinct pure states have disjoint underlying supports. ψ-complete additionally treats the wavefunction as a complete state description; extra variables can make an ontic model incomplete in that sense."}, {"title": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument", "url": "09-quantum-state-ontology.html#exercise-2", "text": "Why must perfectly distinguishable states have disjoint supports? On shared positive support, the response would have to be both one and zero almost everywhere to reproduce the two deterministic predictions, an impossibility."}, {"title": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument", "url": "09-quantum-state-ontology.html#exercise-3", "text": "Is any personal-probability interpretation automatically a PBR model? No. The theorem applies to the specified ontological-model framework. A philosophy of probability need not adopt that underlying representation."}, {"title": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument", "url": "09-quantum-state-ontology.html#exercise-4", "text": "Expand ξ₀₊ in the computational basis. It is (|00⟩−|01⟩+|10⟩+|11⟩)/2."}, {"title": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument", "url": "09-quantum-state-ontology.html#exercise-5", "text": "Expand ξ₊₀ and check its overlap with |+0⟩. It is (|00⟩+|01⟩−|10⟩+|11⟩)/2. The overlap with (|00⟩+|10⟩)/√2 is zero."}, {"title": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument", "url": "09-quantum-state-ontology.html#exercise-6", "text": "Show ξ₀₊ and ξ₊₀ are orthogonal. The numerator is 1−1−1+1=0, divided by four."}, {"title": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument", "url": "09-quantum-state-ontology.html#exercise-7", "text": "If the single-system overlap weight is ω, what is the product-overlap weight under preparation independence? It is ω², positive whenever ω>0. This is why a shared two-system region cannot be ignored."}, {"title": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument", "url": "09-quantum-state-ontology.html#exercise-8", "text": "Identify the exact contradiction on the joint overlap. Each outcome has zero response there because some possible preparation forbids it. Their sum becomes zero, contradicting normalization to one."}, {"title": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument", "url": "09-quantum-state-ontology.html#exercise-9", "text": "Does the exclusion measurement identify every preparation perfectly? No. Outcome ab rules out preparation ab, but it can be compatible with multiple other preparations. Nonorthogonal states have not become perfectly distinguishable."}, {"title": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument", "url": "09-quantum-state-ontology.html#exercise-10", "text": "What must change in the proof for noisy observations? Small probabilities no longer force response functions to vanish. One needs a quantitative bound on overlap from the observed forbidden-outcome probabilities and experimental assumptions."}, {"title": "An ontological-model vocabulary", "chapter": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument", "url": "09-quantum-state-ontology.html#section-9-1", "text": "9.1 An ontological-model vocabulary 9.1.1 Separate an underlying state from its preparation distribution To discuss whether a state describes knowledge, we first need a model in which that sentence has a precise meaning. Let λ denote an underlying physical state. Preparing a pure state ψ produces a distribution μψ(λ). A measurement M has response probabilities ξ(k|M,λ), normalized over k. Reproducing quantum predictions requires \\[\\int d\\lambda\\,\\mu_\\psi(\\lambda)\\xi(k|M,\\lambda)=\\langle\\psi|E_k|\\psi\\rangle.\\] Within this framework, a model is ψ-ontic if distributions for distinct pure states have disjoint support up to measure-zero sets. It is ψ-epistemic if at least two distinct pure states have overlap of positive measure. In the first case, λ determines which pure-state preparation is compatible with it, but λ may contain more information than ψ. These definitions do not classify every use of “information” in philosophy. They presume an underlying state space, preparation distributions, and response functions. They also concern pure-state distinctions; different decompositions of the same mixed density operator pose additional questions. Orthogonal states already need disjoint supports in such a model: a measurement that distinguishes them perfectly cannot assign both certainty zero and certainty one at a shared λ of positive weight. Nonorthogonal states are the interesting test because no single-copy measurement perfectly distinguishes them. Worked check 9.1. If ψ determines only part of λ, can the model still be ψ-ontic? Reasoned solution Yes. Disjoint supports can make ψ recoverable from λ even when λ contains additional variables. Onticity does not imply completeness."}, {"title": "Preparation independence", "chapter": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument", "url": "09-quantum-state-ontology.html#section-9-2", "text": "9.2 Preparation independence 9.2.1 Build the two-copy exclusion experiment Use the two single-qubit preparations |0⟩ and |+⟩. Suppose their distributions overlap on a region of positive weight. Preparation independence assumes that independently prepared systems have a product ontic distribution \\(\\mu_{ab}(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)=\\mu_a(\\lambda_1)\\mu_b(\\lambda_2)\\), with the appropriate composite state space. The shared region then occurs with positive weight for all four preparations 00, 0+, +0, and ++. Now define four orthonormal measurement vectors, with |±⟩=(|0⟩±|1⟩)/√2: \\[\\begin{aligned}|\\xi_{00}\\rangle&=(|01\\rangle+|10\\rangle)/\\sqrt2,\\\\|\\xi_{0+}\\rangle&=(|0-\\rangle+|1+\\rangle)/\\sqrt2,\\\\|\\xi_{+0}\\rangle&=(|+1\\rangle+|-0\\rangle)/\\sqrt2,\\\\|\\xi_{++}\\rangle&=(|+-\\rangle+|-+\\rangle)/\\sqrt2.\\end{aligned}\\] The outcome labelled ab is impossible for preparation ab: \\(\\langle\\xi_{ab}|a,b\\rangle=0\\). This is exclusion, not discrimination. One outcome rules out its associated preparation, but generally does not identify which of the other three was prepared. It is worth expanding these vectors rather than trusting the labels. In particular, the final vector is (|00⟩−|11⟩)/√2. Its overlap with |++⟩ vanishes by cancellation. The four vectors form a projective measurement on the four-dimensional joint space. Worked check 9.2. Verify that ξ₀₊ is orthogonal to |0+⟩. Reasoned solution Its overlap is (⟨−|+⟩+0)/√2=0. Its normalization follows from the orthogonal first-qubit labels in its two terms."}, {"title": "What the contradiction establishes", "chapter": "Quantum-State Ontology and the PBR Argument", "url": "09-quantum-state-ontology.html#section-9-3", "text": "9.3 What the contradiction establishes 9.3.1 Keep the assumptions attached to the conclusion Each impossible quantum outcome has zero average response probability. Because the integrand μξ is nonnegative, the corresponding response must vanish almost everywhere on that preparation's support. On the joint overlap region, all four preparations are possible, so every one of the four response probabilities would have to vanish. That contradicts their required sum of one. This is the two-copy Pusey–Barrett–Rudolph argument for this pair of states. The general result uses suitable numbers of copies for other distinct states. It rules out the relevant overlap under the ontological-model assumptions and preparation independence. Relaxing preparation independence changes the argument and must be evaluated as a concrete alternative assumption. Notice what has not been shown. We have not proved the wavefunction is a material field in three-dimensional space, that it is a complete physical description, or that a particular interpretation is uniquely correct. We have constrained one way of treating pure states as overlapping distributions over a deeper state. Experimental noise also matters: exact zeros become small nonzero probabilities, so a robust inference requires quantitative inequalities rather than repeating the zero-probability proof unchanged. This chapter gives a finite exact example and an assumption audit, not a claim to settle all meanings of quantum reality. Worked check 9.3. Why does nonnegativity matter in the support argument? Reasoned solution A nonnegative function with zero integral vanishes almost everywhere with respect to the preparation measure. Without nonnegativity, cancellations could produce zero while individual responses remained nonzero."}, {"title": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations — Exercise 1", "chapter": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations", "url": "10-quantum-observers.html#exercise-1", "text": "Why is Wigner’s interference test not a routine reading of a notebook? It projects onto superpositions of whole record states and requires coherent control of all relevant record degrees of freedom. Reading the notebook measures the record basis."}, {"title": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations — Exercise 2", "chapter": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations", "url": "10-quantum-observers.html#exercise-2", "text": "For γ=0.2, compute the interference-test probability. It is (1+0.2)/2=0.6. This assumes γ is real in the chosen phase convention."}, {"title": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations — Exercise 3", "chapter": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations", "url": "10-quantum-observers.html#exercise-3", "text": "What happens to the friend’s record under exact reversal? Both correlated branches return the memory to the ready state. The interference test erases the original record rather than preserving it as a simultaneously readable classical fact."}, {"title": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations — Exercise 4", "chapter": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations", "url": "10-quantum-observers.html#exercise-4", "text": "Normalize the three-term two-laboratory state. The squared norm is 3×(1/3)=1, so the stated coefficient 1/√3 is correct."}, {"title": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations — Exercise 5", "chapter": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations", "url": "10-quantum-observers.html#exercise-5", "text": "Calculate the joint amplitude for both ok outcomes. The contributions are +1/√12, −1/√12, and +1/√12. Their sum is 1/√12 and its squared magnitude is 1/12."}, {"title": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations — Exercise 6", "chapter": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations", "url": "10-quantum-observers.html#exercise-6", "text": "What is p(ok|t) in the record-conditioned state? It is zero because ok=(d−u)/√2 is orthogonal to that conditional vector. Transferring this certainty to incompatible observer contexts requires additional justification."}, {"title": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations — Exercise 7", "chapter": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations", "url": "10-quantum-observers.html#exercise-7", "text": "Why can’t the no-go title be read as a universal proof of inconsistency? The contradiction concerns a specified package of quantum predictions, cross-agent certainty rules, and single-outcome commitments. Different approaches can reject or restrict different premises."}, {"title": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations — Exercise 8", "chapter": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations", "url": "10-quantum-observers.html#exercise-8", "text": "Give a fair criterion for empirical equivalence in the final project. The two accounts must assign identical probabilities to every outcome of the specified operational protocols, using their own correctly applied state and dynamics rules."}, {"title": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations — Exercise 9", "chapter": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations", "url": "10-quantum-observers.html#exercise-9", "text": "Name one possible empirical difference for a collapse proposal. A specified collapse law can predict additional interference suppression or diffusion beyond the environmental model. The predicted size must be calculated from its parameters."}, {"title": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations — Exercise 10", "chapter": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations", "url": "10-quantum-observers.html#exercise-10", "text": "Write an assumption audit for the coherent friend test. Assume a pure |+⟩ input, a reversible record interaction, coherent isolation or access to all records, the chosen global projector, and the interpretation’s state-update rules. Removing coherent access changes the experiment and the inference."}, {"title": "Wigner’s friend as an experiment", "chapter": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations", "url": "10-quantum-observers.html#section-10-1", "text": "10.1 Wigner’s friend as an experiment 10.1.1 Specify which laboratory observable is measured Model the friend by a memory with a reversible interaction \\(|0,F_r\\rangle\\mapsto|0,F_0\\rangle\\), \\(|1,F_r\\rangle\\mapsto|1,F_1\\rangle\\). After a |+⟩ input, an outside observer assigns \\(|\\Phi_L^+\\rangle=(|0,F_0\\rangle+|1,F_1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\) to the isolated laboratory. Calling the memory a friend changes the conceptual question, not the linear algebra. Wigner can in principle measure the projector \\(P_+=|\\Phi_L^+\\rangle\\langle\\Phi_L^+|\\). The coherent state predicts p(+)=1. A mixture of the two record branches predicts p(+)=1/2. An equivalent test reverses the friend interaction and measures X on the qubit. The record is then erased; this is not a simultaneous nondisturbing reading of the original record and its incompatible interference observable. For a macroscopic laboratory the required control is demanding because all environmental records must be included or undone. The thought experiment assumes this control. An ordinary observer outside an uncontrolled room cannot simply access that interference by deciding to use a different interpretation. Thus the central issue is the scope of state assignments and collapse claims. A conditional branch state can predict ordinary record comparisons correctly yet fail as a replacement for the full state in a later global interference experiment. The ideal friend model uses a reversible memory. MX denotes X-basis readout; double lines carry a classical result. The second gate erases the original memory. Complete dephasing between the gates changes p(+) from one to one half. Worked check 10.1. For a coherent laboratory with real residual overlap γ, find the P₊ probability. Reasoned solution The effective two-branch state has equal diagonals and off-diagonals γ/2, so p(+)=(1+γ)/2. Complete coherence gives one; full dephasing gives one half."}, {"title": "Cross-agent inference", "chapter": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations", "url": "10-quantum-observers.html#section-10-2", "text": "10.2 Cross-agent inference 10.2.1 Separate a joint amplitude from a chain of certainty claims A useful two-laboratory state for studying the Frauchiger–Renner argument is \\[|\\Psi\\rangle=\\frac{|h,d\\rangle+|t,d\\rangle+|t,u\\rangle}{\\sqrt3}.\\] The first label includes a coin and its friend's record, the second a spin and its friend's record. Define \\(|\\overline{\\mathrm{ok}}\\rangle=(|h\\rangle-|t\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\) and \\(|\\mathrm{ok}\\rangle=(|d\\rangle-|u\\rangle)/\\sqrt2\\). The joint amplitude for the two outside “ok” outcomes is (1−1+1)/√12=1/√12, giving probability 1/12. There is also a tempting inference chain. Conditioning on t leaves (d+u)/√2, which predicts the second outside outcome “fail” with certainty. Conditioning on u implies t. Projecting the first laboratory onto overline-ok leaves a vector proportional to u, suggesting that this observer can adopt the second friend's certainty, and then the first friend's certainty, to infer “fail.” Yet the joint Born calculation allows both outside “ok” outcomes. The mistake is not in the three-term amplitude. The tension concerns transferring certainty statements across agents, conditioning contexts, and incompatible later measurements. The published no-go argument formalizes a package involving quantum predictions, consistency of agents' certainty inferences, and single outcomes. Analyses disagree about which step an interpretation should reject. We derive the finite-state calculation here; a complete logical theorem and its variants require the source's precise rules. Worked check 10.2. Find p(overline-ok) and p(ok|overline-ok) for the three-term state. Reasoned solution Projection cancels the d terms and leaves −|u⟩/√6, so p(overline-ok)=1/6. In |u⟩ the final ok probability is 1/2. Their product is 1/12."}, {"title": "A comparative research practicum", "chapter": "Quantum Observers and Comparing Interpretations", "url": "10-quantum-observers.html#section-10-3", "text": "10.3 A comparative research practicum 10.3.1 Evaluate accounts with the same mathematical benchmark We can now compare approaches without asking a popularity question. Use one laboratory interaction, one initial state, and two possible later measurements: a record comparison and a coherent interference test. Then ask each account what is actual, what evolves, what the state describes, and when a conditional state is legitimate. Questions to carry into the final comparison; these are broad families, not exhaustive definitions. Approach Central commitment Explanatory task to examine Operational accounts Probabilities for specified procedures Scope and physical status of the measurement description Consistent histories Probabilities inside consistent frameworks Framework selection and incompatible propositions Everett Universal unitary state, relative records Branch structure and probability justification Bohmian mechanics Wavefunction plus actual configuration Equilibrium, nonlocality, and effective collapse Objective collapse Additional stochastic localization law Ontology, parameter constraints, and deviations Relational mechanics Events relative to physical systems Relations between records and facts QBism Agent probabilities for consequences of actions Shared science and normative quantum constraints For the final project, choose two approaches and write a short technical comparison. Derive the probabilities of both later measurements, label every use of conditioning, and identify which assumption in the measurement-problem package each approach changes. If the approaches are empirically equivalent for this experiment, say so. Do not invent a distinguishing experiment by silently assigning one of them the wrong state. Include one reproducible calculation, a primary-source passage discussed in your own words, a serious objection and response for each account, and a statement of what remains unresolved. A strong conclusion can be conditional: “Under these assumptions the predictions agree, while the explanatory commitments differ.” That is a substantive result. The numerical companion checks the finite examples in this course; it does not certify an interpretation. Worked check 10.3. What would make a claimed experimental distinction invalid? Reasoned solution Giving one empirically equivalent account a collapsed mixture where its own rules retain a coherent full state. Predictions must be derived from each account’s actual commitments and the same physical protocol."}];
